{% comment %}
  Resolves a filter value's display label from its backing metaobject entry
  rather than trusting Shopify's `value.label`.

  Why: for metaobject-reference filters, `value.label` is served from Shopify's
  storefront filter index, which mirrors the Search & Discovery "Label" field
  mapping only after an internal re-derivation the shop cannot trigger. Observed
  live on this shop (2026-08-26): after both the `activities` and `filtercolors`
  filters were switched in Search & Discovery from the `code` field to the
  `label` field, the index kept serving the old English `code` text for Kleur
  ("black", "blue") and a stale humanized handle for one activity ("Hiking"
  rather than "Wandelen"), while the app's own Values screen showed the correct
  Dutch labels. Touching products to force a reindex changed nothing.

  The metaobject entry itself is always correct and is already read from Liquid
  by ob-facet-color-chip to resolve the chip hex, so the label is read from the
  same place. The rendered label is then correct regardless of what the filter
  index serves, and stays correct once it catches up.

  Matching is by metaobject GID only; the label is read from `label_nl`, the
  field the Akeneo sync writes the Dutch text into. If it ever renders English
  again, read trap 5 below before changing anything — the field goes unreadable
  after a connector definition rewrite and the fix is to recreate it, not to
  rename it. A facet whose value is not a metaobject GID (vendor, product_type,
  price, the size list) short-circuits on the `contains` guard and keeps
  `value.label`.

  Output is already HTML-escaped — callers MUST NOT escape it again.

  Accepts:
  - value: {Object} a filter value object (from facets.liquid)

  Usage:
  {%- capture value_label -%}
    {%- render 'ob-facet-value-label', value: value -%}
  {%- endcapture -%}
{% endcomment %}
{%- comment -%}
  Five Liquid traps, every one confirmed live by instrumenting the rendered
  page. Keep this snippet exactly as written — each looks harmless and each
  silently degrades to the fallback rather than erroring:

  1. `shop.metaobjects[some_variable]` resolves to nil, so each metaobject type
     must be named statically; a new metaobject-backed facet needs its own loop.
  2. `shop.metaobjects.<type>.values` is a lazy drop. `.size` reports correctly
     (15), but `.first` returns nil and assigning the collection to a variable
     before iterating produces an empty loop — it must be iterated directly.
  3. A metaobject entry has NO `.id` property; it reads as nil, so comparing
     `entry.id` to anything never matches.
  4. `entry.system.id` is the BARE NUMERIC id (`224312459373`) while a filter
     value's `value.value` is the full GID
     (`gid://shopify/Metaobject/224312459373`), so the prefix must be
     re-attached before comparing.
  5. **A metaobject field can become permanently unreadable on the storefront
     while looking perfectly healthy everywhere else.** Symptom: `entry.<key>`
     returns the entry's HANDLE instead of the stored value. Observed
     2026-08-31, twice. Best explanation — inferred from a single occurrence,
     not reproduced: when the Akeneo connector rewrites the metaobject
     *definition* (it prunes fields no longer in its mapping), fields that
     survive the rewrite are left orphaned in Shopify's storefront index and
     stop serving their values. Treat the symptom and the fix below as the
     reliable part; treat the cause as a working theory.

     How to tell the two failure modes apart:
     - key returns **empty** -> the field does not exist on the definition
     - key returns **the handle** -> the field exists but is orphaned

     Everything else looks fine and will mislead you: the Admin API returns the
     correct value, `access.storefront` is `PUBLIC_READ`, `.type` reports
     `single_line_text_field`, there are no translations overriding it, and
     sibling fields on the same entry (`code`, `hexcode`) read correctly.

     **Fix: delete the field from the definition, re-create it, re-write the
     values.** It works immediately — no waiting, no reindex. Nothing else
     worked: writing a sentinel value did not propagate, and waiting did not
     help. See NICK.md item 4 for the exact mutations.

     This also explains the earlier `label` / `display_label` failures, which
     were misdiagnosed for days as "reserved Liquid property names". They were
     orphaned the same way. Field *naming* is NOT the issue — `label_nl` itself
     broke after the connector's 15:02 sync and was restored by recreating it.

     **Any Akeneo mapping change by Nick can re-break this.** If the facets
     suddenly render English again, this is the first thing to check.

  Trap 5 masked every earlier attempt: the GID match was succeeding all along,
  but the orphaned field handed back the handle, which for most colours is
  identical to the English fallback and so looked like "no match".
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign ob_gid_prefix = 'gid://shopify/Metaobject/' -%}
{%- assign ob_resolved_label = '' -%}

{%- if value.value contains ob_gid_prefix -%}
  {%- for ob_entry in shop.metaobjects.filtercolors.values -%}
    {%- assign ob_entry_gid = ob_gid_prefix | append: ob_entry.system.id -%}
    {%- if ob_entry_gid == value.value -%}
      {%- capture ob_resolved_label %}{{ ob_entry.label_nl }}{% endcapture -%}
      {%- break -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}

  {%- if ob_resolved_label == '' -%}
    {%- for ob_entry in shop.metaobjects.activities.values -%}
      {%- assign ob_entry_gid = ob_gid_prefix | append: ob_entry.system.id -%}
      {%- if ob_entry_gid == value.value -%}
        {%- capture ob_resolved_label %}{{ ob_entry.label_nl }}{% endcapture -%}
        {%- break -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- assign ob_resolved_label = ob_resolved_label | strip -%}
{%- if ob_resolved_label == '' -%}
  {%- assign ob_resolved_label = value.label -%}
{%- endif -%}
{{- ob_resolved_label | escape -}}
